= Roadmap

There are no dates. Each milestone has *exit criteria*. A milestone is done when its gallery documents open in Word with no repair prompt, pass both validators, and match their golden snapshots.

== M0: Foundations

- \[x\] Repository setup: `uv`, `ruff`, `pyright --strict`, `pytest`, GitHub Actions (Windows, Linux, macOS), license, CONTRIBUTING.
- \[x\] OPC package writer: content types, relationships, deterministic zip.
- \[x\] Minimal part writers: document, styles (docDefaults, the four default styles, user styles), settings, core and app props, fontTable, theme (own default theme).
- \[x\] Validation pipeline: lxml + ECMA-376 Transitional XSDs (now `src/redocx/data/schemas/`); `tools/ooxml-validate` (.NET OpenXmlValidator).
- \[x\] Golden-test harness (canonical XML snapshots in `tests/golden/expected/`).
- \[x\] First version of the property registry: 15 run and 14 paragraph properties, with schema-order tables checked against `wml.xsd`.
- \[x\] *Spike:* 5 representative documents in the proposed syntax. See the findings.
- *Exit:* the Python API produces a valid document with styled paragraphs. This is met locally: the golden cases pass both validators and open in Word 365 with the expected formatting. CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs on pushes to `main` and on pull requests.

== M1: Core language (roughly "Pandoc parity")

- \[x\] Parser for all modes, lossless CST, typed AST, source spans, diagnostics renderer (`redocx.syntax`; rules in design §3.22). Checked by property-based fuzzing (Hypothesis) and a syntax tour (`examples/syntax-tour.rdx`).
- \[x\] CLI `redocx check` and `redocx parse` (`--tree ast|cst`).
- \[x\] Lowering AST -> IR (`redocx.lower`, design §3.23): inline sugar, formatting commands, `\p`, `\align`, option type-checking, units and colours (incl. theme `lighter`/`darker`), smart punctuation.
- \[x\] Style sheets (`.rdxs` and `\styles{}`): defaults, paragraph, character and linked styles, theme references, modify/replace, multilevel numbering definitions with style-linked levels (`numbering.xml`). Table styles are parsed but deferred to M2.
- \[x\] Headings, paragraphs, lists with real multilevel numbering (separate lists restart; `\list[Name, continue]`, `restart=N`).
- \[x\] Single-section page setup, page and column breaks, hyperlinks.
- \[x\] Inline images (PNG, JPEG, GIF, BMP) and tables: pipe and block forms, widths, horizontal and vertical merges, shading, header rows, row height, nested tables (borders via `Table Grid`; custom table styles in M2).
- \[x\] `\include`, `\use` (style sheets and macro files), `\let`, `\define` (macros with `#param`), `\settings`, `\document`, `\meta`.
- \[x\] Command registry with argument kinds (markup, string, raw, math); the comment, whitespace and flanking rules from the spike.
- \[x\] Standard macro library (`std:callout`, `std:signature`, `std:latex`), packaged with the compiler and loaded by default.
- \[x\] Trial: the spike's design specification ported to v0.2 (`examples/design-spec/`, findings in its `NOTES.md`).
- \[x\] CLI: `redocx build` (and `check` runs the full compile).
- *Exit:* a real 20-page document can be authored entirely in redocx. Language grammar v0.1 is frozen. _Status:_ the 4-page specification trial builds cleanly, and its remaining gaps (headers and footers, TOC, cross-references, captions, footnotes, sections) are M2 items. The grammar has been stable through M1 (v0.2 plus the §3.22–3.23 rules) and through M2–M7, and was frozen at 0.1.0 (Q14).

== M2: Word structure (where redocx goes beyond Pandoc)

- \[x\] Multiple sections (orientation, size, margins, columns, break types); headers and footers (first, even, linked or not); page-number formats and restarts; fields (`\pagenum`, `\numpages`, `\field`, `\date`); tab stops.
- \[x\] Bookmarks; cross-references with pre-computed text (`@label`, `\ref`, `\pageref`); captions (`\caption`, `\figure`, code-block captions); `\seq` counters; TOC and lists of figures/tables with pre-rendered entries.
- \[x\] Footnotes and endnotes (`\footnote`, `\endnote`, labels and `NOTEREF`, numbering format, start, restart and position settings).
- \[x\] Full tables: merges, borders, shading (theme colours), repeat headers, cell margins, look flags, table styles with conditional formatting (header row, last row, first/last column, banded rows/columns, corner cells).
- \[x\] Templates: compile into a `.dotx` or `.docx` (styles, numbering, theme, page setup, headers and footers, body content, other parts); write `.dotx` templates.
- \[x\] Heading numbering, legal numbering, tab stops, paragraph borders (sides, between, bar) and shading.
- \[x\] Document properties: core, custom (`\meta[custom.NAME = ...]`, merged with a template's) and `DOCPROPERTY`; general `\field`.
- \[x\] Sweep: document protection, mirror margins, line numbering, section vertical alignment, decimal table columns, chapter-numbered captions, references in headers and footers, per-cell margins and text direction, more run and paragraph properties (scale, kerning, emboss, imprint, outline, shadow, suppress line numbers and hyphenation), and the escape hatches (`\raw[ooxml]`, `ooxml.*` properties). Moved out of M2: list (numbering) styles (later), columns of different widths (M4).
- *Exit:* redocx's own design docs are rebuilt in redocx with a corporate-style template and look professionally typeset in Word. _Status: met._ `docs/book/design.rdx` builds the eight design documents into one 38-page book with the `docs/book/redocx.dotx` template (itself written in redocx): cover page, contents, numbered chapters with running heads, styled tables with repeated header rows, boxed code, notes, front matter with roman page numbers. It builds without diagnostics, passes both validators and opens cleanly in Word. The chapters are converted from the Markdown sources by `tools/md2rdx.py`, and the test suite keeps them in step.

== M3: Technical writing

Steps, in order:

1. \[x\] *Math* (LaTeX subset to OMML, `redocx.math`). Inline `\m{…}`, display `$$…$$` and `\math[…]{…}`. Fractions, roots, scripts, big operators with limits, delimiters (`\left…\right`), functions and `\lim`, accents and bars, matrices and `cases`, `aligned` rows with `&` alignment, text and font styles (`\mathbf`, `\mathbb`, …), Greek letters and the common symbol set. Unsupported macros are errors at their position. OMML is written the way Word writes it (the reference survey settled Q9). A numbered display equation is an `m:eqArr` with Word's own `#` number. Labelled equations are numbered, optionally per chapter, and `@eq:x` prints "(3.2)".
2. \[x\] *Code highlighting* (Pygments): token runs in generated, themeable character styles (`Code Keyword`, …), `line-numbers`, captions as now.
3. \[x\] *Citations and bibliography*: BibTeX or CSL-JSON data, CSL styles through citeproc-py, `\cite`/`\textcite` linked to entries, `\printbibliography` in a `Bibliography` style. Bundled styles: Harvard, APA, IEEE, Chicago author-date.
4. \[x\] *Index*: `\ix{term!sub}` as `XE` fields, `\index[columns=2]` as an `INDEX` field with pre-rendered entries.
5. \[x\] *Images and embedding*: SVG (with a PNG fallback, as Word stores it: `fallback=` or a grey placeholder), `\embed-docx` and `\embed-rtf` (altChunk, merged by Word on open), and `\include-rtf` (an RTF file read into native content; the same reader imports .rtf).
6. \[x\] *Tooling*: `redocx fmt` (CST-based formatter, `--check`, `--diff`; §3.21), `--emit-ir` (the IR as JSON, one-way).
- *Exit:* a thesis-style document with math, citations, code and figures. _Status: met._ `examples/thesis/thesis.rdx` is a 13-page MSc thesis in five included chapter files with its own style sheet: title page, abstract, contents, lists of figures, tables and listings, front matter with roman page numbers, chapters with running heads, numbered equations referenced per chapter ("(2–1)"), APA citations and references, highlighted code listings, an SVG and a PNG figure, tables with repeated header rows, footnotes and a two-column index. It builds without diagnostics, passes both validators, and after Word updates its fields the contents, lists, index and references are all right. Building it found two bugs, both fixed: the equation bookmark covered the parentheses, so Word's update of a reference showed "((2.1))", and a chapter separator other than "." was set as math, spacing an en dash like a minus sign.

== M4: Advanced layout

Steps, in order (the small ones first; the drawing steps share one positioning model):

1. \[x\] *Page decoration*: page borders on sections (`borders=`), the page colour (`\document[page-color=…]`), borders and shading on runs.
2. \[x\] *Custom themes*: `theme [Name] { accent1: …; major-font: …; }` in a style sheet sets the theme's colours and fonts (redocx's theme part or the template's), so `theme.accent1` and `theme.major` mean the document's own choices. Tints and shades are computed as Word computes them (HSL luminance).
3. \[x\] *Floating images*: `\image[wrap=…, x=…, y=…]` as `wp:anchor` (square, tight, top-and-bottom, behind, in front), positioned against the page, margin, column or paragraph; crop, rotation and a border on any image.
4. \[x\] *Text boxes and watermarks*: `\textbox` (a `wps` shape with markup inside, inline or floating, with fill and border), `\watermark` (text or a picture behind every page of a section, in the header).
5. \[x\] *Frames and floating tables*: drop caps (`w:framePr`; other text frames are postponed), floating tables (`w:tblpPr`), columns of different widths.
6. \[x\] *Right-to-left and complex scripts*: right-to-left paragraphs, tables, runs and sections; complex-script and East Asian fonts and languages; bold and italic for complex scripts; vertical sections.

Basic shapes (lines, arrows, rectangles) come only if they stay small; see Postponed features.

- *Exit:* a magazine-style report: a cover page with a full-bleed picture, floating figures with text around them, a pull quote in a text box, a drop cap, a "DRAFT" watermark and its own theme. _Status: met._ `examples/magazine/magazine.rdx` is a three-page review: a full-bleed cover behind white Georgia titles; a two-column feature with a page-wide picture at the top, a drop cap in the heading font, a pull quote floating across the columns, a tightly wrapped picture, a floating table, a sidebar box and an SVG figure; a running head, a page frame and a DRAFT watermark, all in its own Harbour theme. It builds without diagnostics, passes both validators and lays out in Word as intended. Building it changed two things: percentage widths in a section with columns are now of the column, and a drop cap skips floating objects anchored at the start of its paragraph.

== M5: Collaboration & forms

Steps, in order. Each stays simple: a part that turns out intrusive (new parts, Word-version extensions, changes all over the writer) goes to Postponed features instead.

1. \[x\] *Settings*: `\settings[track-changes]` (Word's Track Changes on for edits made later in Word) and `hyphenation=auto` (left over from M4).
2. \[x\] *Comments*: `\comment[author=…, initials=…, date=…, text={…}]{commented text}`: the range, the reference mark and `comments.xml`; markup in the comment; a point comment without a body. Replies and the resolved state are left out.
3. \[x\] *Content controls*: `\control[type=…, tag=…, title=…, lock=…]`, inline or around blocks: plain and rich text, dropdown and combo box, date, checkbox; placeholder text; with the existing form protection.
4. \[x\] *Picture controls* (an image, or an empty frame). Repeating sections are postponed (Word 2013's `w15` markup and a section that repeats are more than they give).
- *Exit:* a fillable onboarding form: every field type, form protection, a reviewer's comments, Track Changes on. _Status: met._ `examples/forms/onboarding.rdx` is a one-page form protected for filling in: text, dropdown, combo, date, picture, rich-text and checkbox fields with placeholders, a locked field, a reviewer's comments (one with markup) and Track Changes on. It builds without diagnostics and passes both validators; in Word all fourteen controls read back with their types, filling a field, a dropdown and a checkbox works under protection, the comments show in the margin, and once unprotected, edits are tracked.

Tracked revisions in the source and `redocx diff` redlines are postponed; data binding stays "Later".

== M6: Import (`docx → redocx`)

The goal is a *reliable import of the significant part of a document and its formatting*, not a round trip: some data is inevitably stripped or approximated, and the import says what (no silent loss). `redocx import doc.docx` writes a folder: `doc.rdx`, a style sheet, the pictures, and a report. The reader (`.docx` → IR, behind `redocx inspect`) exists since M1; the work is mostly the other half, printing IR as readable source, plus filling the reader's gaps. The thinking behind it is in the decompiler note.

Steps, in order. Each stays simple; what turns out intrusive is reported as lost and listed in Postponed features.

1. \[x\] *Measure and fill the reader's gaps.* Build a small public corpus of Word-authored test documents (made through Word itself, so they are ours to commit) and run `inspect` on them and, locally, on the user's real test document. Count what the reader drops, by construct, and teach it the most frequent ones first, starting with what the writer already supports: sections with headers and footers, notes, comments, fields (TOC, REF, SEQ, PAGE), bookmarks, numbering, tables with merges, floating pictures, text boxes, content controls. _Status:_ `tools/corpus/make-corpus.ps1` drives Word to build six documents in `tests/corpus` (text, tables, references, sections, drawings, review), and `tests/ooxml/test_corpus.py` checks what each holds is read, that the only losses are the expected ones, and that the IR read back writes a valid package. The reader now reads notes, comments, block fields (the TOC), table styles with their conditions, table and cell borders and margins, paragraph and run borders and shading, floating pictures with crop, border and rotation, text boxes, drop caps, EMF/WMF previews of embedded objects, page borders and section columns. On the private test document the fallbacks went from 557 to 48 (text effects, orphan bookmark ends, emphasis marks, one embedded object), and what is read writes a package both validators accept.
2. \[x\] *Print canonical source (L1).* IR → `.rdx` with commands only: `\p[style=…]`, direct formatting as `\font[…]`, `\table`/`\row`/`\cell`, `\section`, `\header`/`\footer`, `\footnote`, `\image` (pictures extracted), fields as their commands or `\field`. Escaping, and units snapped to what a person writes (1cm, 12pt). Anything not printable becomes a line in the report. The check: the printed source compiles without errors and has the original's text. _Status:_ `redocx import doc.docx [-o DIR]` (`redocx.importer`) writes `doc.rdx`, `doc.rdxs`, `media/` and `doc.import.txt`, then compiles the printed source and compares its text with the original's (every run, notes, comments, text boxes, headers and footers; whitespace, field results and the TOC's entries left out). Lists become `\list[Name]{1. …}` with `continue` and `restart=`, comments `\comment[text={…}]{…}`, drop caps `\p[drop-cap=N]`, TOC fields `\toc`/`\listof`, other block fields a `\field` Word fills in, bookmarks raw XML (the hidden `_Toc` and `_GoBack` ones are dropped). Text is escaped only where it would read as markup, with smart quotes off so it stays as typed. The style sheet declares the styles the document uses, their bases and next styles, its lists and its defaults, as read. Language additions it needed: `\link[to="#bookmark"]`, `line-spacing=(14pt, exact)` as a `\p` option, EMF and WMF pictures in `\image`. Reader fixes it found: pictures in field results (`LINK`, `INCLUDEPICTURE`), theme colours of borders, two lists of one name. The six corpus documents and 13 examples (built, then imported) compile with the same text; so does the private test document (locally: no errors, the same text, the same tables, pictures and sections in Word; 19 pages instead of 17, since it now has redocx's theme and settings rather than its own).
3. \[x\] *Styles.* The style sheet from step 2, made readable: printing only what differs from redocx's built-ins, linked styles as `linked`, the theme's colours and fonts. Alternative mode: keep the original's styles, theme, numbering and headers exactly by writing them as a body-less template the source compiles into (`\document[template=…]`). _Status:_ the reader reads the theme, and the style sheet has a `theme` rule with the colours and fonts that differ from redocx's. A style redocx has built in becomes `modify paragraph "Heading 1" { … }` with only what differs, when its own definition can be extended to it (a style sheet cannot take a property away); a paragraph style whose "… Char" style formats text the same way becomes one `linked` rule. Where the original's defaults leave out the font or size, the sheet writes Word's legacy Times New Roman 10pt, since redocx would fill in its own (this was the private document's two extra pages). `redocx import --keep-styles` writes the original without its body (and without the final section's header references) as `doc.template.docx`, and the source compiles into it with no style sheet: templates now know unnamed lists by the names the reader gives them ("List 3"). The private test document comes back at its 17 pages either way.
4. \[x\] *Clean up and sugar (L2).* Merge Word's run soup into runs that differ in effective formatting, drop proofing and revision-id noise, and use the language's own forms where the result compiles to the same thing: `=` headings, `-` and `1.` lists, `*strong*`/`_emph_`, pipe tables, `{#label}` and `@label` from bookmarks and REF fields, `\toc`, captions from SEQ fields. _Status:_ the import prints sugar by default (`--commands-only` gives L1). Direct formatting that repeats what the paragraph's style gives is left out (outside tables, whose styles format text too), and runs that end up alike merge. Headings without direct formatting are `=` lines; bold, italic, underline, strike, superscript, subscript and hidden text are `\b{…}` … `\hidden{…}`; the Strong and Emphasis styles are `*…*` and `_…_` where the words around allow it. Table rows of plain one-paragraph cells are pipe rows (`header-rows=N` for the leading header rows), inside `\table[…]`. `importer.labels` finds the bookmarks REF and PAGEREF fields point at: one around a whole heading becomes `{#sec:…}`, and in a Caption-style paragraph written as `Figure {SEQ Figure}: text` the caption becomes `\caption[Figure, id=fig:…]{text}`; the fields become `@label`, `\ref[form=text|full|number]{…}` and `\pageref{…}`, and hidden `_Ref` bookmarks nothing points at are dropped. Lists were already `\list[Name]{1. …}`, the TOC `\toc`. Each form is used only where it compiles to the same thing: the tests compare, for the corpus and the examples, every paragraph's style, formatting and list level, every table's spans and header rows, and the effective formatting of every run. They found two step-2 bugs, now fixed: links without the Hyperlink style (redocx's citations) came back blue, so `\link` takes `style=none`; and paragraphs in tables in headers and footers took the Header and Footer styles. The private test document: the same structure (300 blocks) and look (every body paragraph); 30 headings are `=` lines, and `\font[…]` goes from 125 to 74.
5. \[x\] *The oracle.* `redocx import --check` compiles the imported source and compares it with the original: text, block structure, paragraph styles and effective character formatting, reported as a share of paragraphs that match and a list of differences. Round-trip tests on the examples (`.rdx → .docx → import → .docx`) keep it honest. _Status:_ `redocx.importer.check` (moved out of step 4's tests) compares the text, the blocks (each paragraph's style, formatting and list level, each table's spans and header rows), the effective run formatting of each body paragraph (resolved with the original's styles when the source compiles into it with `--keep-styles`) and the counts of sections, notes, comments, pictures and text boxes. Every import reports "Blocks that match the original: N of M", the same for paragraphs, and the first differences; `--check` makes any difference fail the command. Known losses are not counted (drop caps, a regenerated TOC, an INDEX that comes back as its field, headers Word does not show). Layout is Word's and is not compared. The tests run it on the six corpus documents and all 13 examples, in both modes.
- *Exit:* the user's real test document imports with its text complete, its structure (headings, lists, tables, sections, notes) and most of its formatting, the report lists what was lost, and the recompiled document is checked in Word. That document is private: it is only read locally, and neither it nor its imported output is committed or published; committed tests use the public corpus. _Status: met._ Locally, in both modes: it compiles, its text is the original's, 300 of 300 blocks and 46 of 46 body paragraphs match, and Word lays out 17 pages, as for the original, with no field errors. The report lists what was lost (repeated spaces, two even-page parts Word does not show, text effects, emphasis marks, objects kept as pictures).

== M7: Reliability and documentation

_Goal: a document that compiles without errors opens in Word without complaint, and the language can be learned from its documentation._

1. \[x\] *Every example validated.* The examples and the book are built and validated by the schemas and by the Open XML SDK in the tests. This found one bug: math in a document compiled into a template wrote `m:mathPr` out of schema order.
2. \[x\] *Escape hatches checked against the schemas.* `\raw` and `ooxml.*` XML must fit where it goes; the schemas ship with the package and load only when raw XML is used.
3. \[x\] *The written document checked.* Relationship ids, bookmark pairs, unique bookmark names and drawing ids, links to bookmarks that exist, and note, comment, style and list references, as one warning (`RDX0401`); `build --validate` for the full schema check. It found that `[text](#label)` linked to the label's name rather than its bookmark; such links now go to the bookmark.
4. \[x\] *Unknown field names* in `\field` (and its nested fields) are a warning, with the closest known name.
5. \[x\] *Command reference* (docs/reference.md) generated by `tools/make_reference.py` from the command table and the property registry; every option now has a description, and a test keeps the file up to date and every command in a topic.
6. \[x\] *Guides* (docs/guide): a one-page guide; topic guides for syntax, styles and templates, tables, pictures, references, sections and fields, math and code, macros and the standard library, forms, and import; and a guide for TeX users. Every `rdx` example in them compiles without a warning (tested). Writing them corrected two option names in the design document (`\font[font=…]`, `character-spacing`).
7. \[x\] *`redocx explain NAME`:* a command, formatting property, macro or `std:` module from the same tables as the reference (the catalogue is shared with `tools/make_reference.py`); `--in FILE.rdx` adds the macros a document defines or loads, found by parsing and following `\use` and `\include`, with the `%` comment above each definition; `--properties KIND` lists one kind of property.
- *Exit:* a document that compiles without errors opens in Word without complaint, or is reported; and the language can be learned from its guides and looked up in its reference. _Status: met._

Tooling (language server, editor support, `watch`, PDF export and `finalize`, a website) waits until the language has settled: see tooling ideas.

== M8: Generation

_Goal: a program or template engine can write redocx source from any data and compile it safely._

1. \[x\] *Escaping* (`redocx.escape`): `text`, `inline`, `string` and `value` make any data read back as itself in markup, string bodies and option values. The importer's printer uses the same functions.
2. \[x\] *Guide* (Generating documents) with a tested example generator (`examples/generated/invoice.py`) and Jinja filters.
3. \[x\] *CLI for programs*: `build -` and `check -` read standard input, `-o -` writes standard output, `--warnings-as-errors`, `--diagnostics json` (one JSON object per line).
4. \[x\] *A root for file names*: `--root DIR` (`root=` in `redocx.compiler`) makes a file the document names outside one folder an error (`RDX0313`), for sources that hold data from outside.
- *Exit:* the invoice example builds from data with braces, marks and line-start markers in it, and every diagnostic reaches a program as JSON. _Status: met._

== M9: Import of math

_Goal: equations in a `.docx` come back as LaTeX that compiles to the same equation, and nothing is dropped._

1. \[x\] *Raw OMML* in the language: `\ooxml{…}` inside a formula, whole equations in `\raw[ooxml]`, both checked against the schemas (Q15).
2. \[x\] *Reader* (`math/read.py`): OMML into the math tree. What the tree cannot hold (`m:box`, a run with a font size) is kept as OMML, one object at a time.
3. \[x\] *Printer* (`math/latex.py`): the math tree as LaTeX. Each object is parsed back and compared, and written as `\ooxml{…}` when it does not come back the same.
4. \[x\] *Corpus*: `tests/corpus/math.docx`, equations Word built from its linear format.
5. \[x\] *Numbers and labels*: Word's numbered equation (`#(1)` in an equation array) reads as a numbered equation; one a REF points at gets `{#eq:…}`, the REF `@eq:…`. Numbers per chapter set `captions=(chapter-numbers)`. The oracle compares every equation.
- *Exit:* the math example and the Word corpus import with every equation the same as the original's. _Status: met._ Building them found two writer bugs, both fixed: aligned rows were marked with `m:aln`, which Word ignores (they never aligned), and equations had no `m:` prefix in the written XML.

== Later / candidates

Native charts, native Word bibliography, content-control data binding, embedded fonts, building blocks. Postponed indefinitely: see Postponed features.

== Still to do

M0–M9 are done. What is left from them: the public Python API sketched in architecture §4.8 is not built. Programs write source (see M8), or use the IR (`redocx.ir`) and the compiler (`redocx.compiler`) directly.

After that, the tooling ideas, the "Later" candidates above, and the known gaps as documents need them.
